{"note": "GUIDANCE ONLY. Not evidence. Cite ids in knowledge_citations. Never use numbers from sections marked invented_example.", "sections": [{"id": "KB32", "section": "Chapter 10: Positioning, Messaging and Brand Voice > Features, Benefits and Outcomes", "invented_example": false, "text": "A feature is a property of the product, a benefit is what the feature does for the customer, and an outcome is the change in the customer's life or work that results. A jacket that packs into its own pocket has a feature of compact stowage, the benefit of easy carrying, and the outcome of being prepared for changing weather without a heavy bag. Messaging tends to be strongest when it leads with benefits and outcomes and uses features as supporting evidence."}, {"id": "KB33", "section": "Chapter 10: Positioning, Messaging and Brand Voice > Claim Substantiation and Compliance", "invented_example": false, "text": "Advertising claims are generally expected to be truthful and supported by evidence at the time they are made. Regulators in many jurisdictions apply this principle, and platforms enforce their own advertising policies. Claims about performance, health, safety, environmental benefit and comparisons with competitors receive particular scrutiny.

Some terms have technical meanings that differ from everyday usage. In outdoor apparel, for example, water-resistant, water-repellent and waterproof describe different levels of protection. Waterproof implies protection under sustained or heavy exposure, and it is commonly associated with tested ratings and sealed construction, while water-resistant implies protection against light exposure. Thresholds and test methods differ among manufacturers and regions. Using the stronger term for a product that qualifies only for the weaker one creates both legal exposure and customer disappointment, which then appears in reviews and returns."}, {"id": "KB34", "section": "Chapter 10: Positioning, Messaging and Brand Voice > Positioning and Value Propositions", "invented_example": false, "text": "Positioning defines the place a product occupies in the mind of the target customer relative to alternatives. A positioning statement typically names the target audience, the category, the main benefit and the reason to believe it. A value proposition expresses the promised benefit in a form customers can grasp quickly, and it is only credible when supported by evidence in the product and in the experience of other customers.

Good positioning is specific. Statements that apply equally to every competitor, such as high quality or great value, differentiate nothing. Specific claims, such as a particular weight, a particular use case or a particular guarantee, give customers something to evaluate."}]}

