{"KB1": {"chapter": "Chapter 4: Pricing, Discounts and Promotions", "section": "Worked Example: Evaluating a Seven-Day Sale", "score": 0.28061637, "is_invented_example": true, "text": "A hypothetical tea merchant runs a seven-day sale at 20 percent off. In the 28 days before the sale, the merchant averaged 100 orders per day at an average order value of $40 and a gross margin of 55 percent, which means $4,000 of revenue and $2,200 of gross profit per day.\n\n| Period | Orders per day | AOV | Revenue per day | Gross margin | Gross profit per day |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline (28 days) | 100 | $40 | $4,000 | 55% | $2,200 |\n| Sale (7 days) | 180 | $34 | $6,120 | 40% | $2,448 |\n| After the sale (14 days) | 88 | $40 | $3,520 | 55% | $1,936 |\n\nDuring the sale, revenue per day rose by 53 percent, from $4,000 to $6,120, while gross profit per day rose only 11.3 percent, from $2,200 to $2,448. Over seven days, the incremental revenue is $2,120 × 7 = $14,840 and the incremental gross profit is $248 × 7 = $1,736.\n\nThe fourteen days after the sale tell a different story. Revenue ran $480 per day below baseline, a shortfall of $6,720, and gross profit ran $264 per day below baseline, a shortfall of $3,696. Combining the three periods, revenue over 21 days is $8,120 above baseline, about 9.7 percent, while gross profit is $1,960 below baseline, about 4.2 percent.\n\nThe same sale appears successful or unsuccessful depending on the metric and the window. Examining the customer mix refines the picture. Of the 1,260 orders during the sale, suppose 900 came from existing customers and 360 from customers who had never bought before. If each of those 360 new customers goes on to generate $15 of gross profit over the following ninety days, that adds $5,400, which turns a $1,960 loss into a gain of $3,440. Whether the new customers repeat is therefore the deciding uncertainty, and it can only be observed after the fact."}, "KB3": {"chapter": "Chapter 4: Pricing, Discounts and Promotions", "section": "Common Misreadings", "score": 0.12765263, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A frequent misreading reports revenue lift as success. Another compares a promotion with the preceding week without recognizing that the preceding week may have been unusually strong or weak. A third attributes all sales during the window to the promotion, although a large share would have occurred anyway."}}

