Relevant handbook guidance (methodology only):
{"KB3": {"chapter": "Chapter 5: Customer Segmentation and Lifetime Value", "section": "Characteristics of High-Value Customers", "score": 0.21469143, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "When analysts profile the top segment of a customer base, they typically examine several dimensions: the categories and products purchased, the acquisition channel, the frequency and regularity of orders, the average basket size, the share of orders placed with a discount code, the return rate and the tenure. Differences between the top segment and the rest of the base indicate what distinguishes valuable customers.\n\nA recurring observation across many businesses is that the most valuable customers buy from several categories, repeat at steady intervals, and respond less to price promotions than to novelty, availability and recognition. Whether this holds in a specific dataset is an empirical question, and the profile is usually checked against the data instead of assumed."}, "KB4": {"chapter": "Chapter 5: Customer Segmentation and Lifetime Value", "section": "Segmentation Beyond Transactions", "score": 0.17217185, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Surveys, support interactions and browsing behavior add information that orders do not hold, such as motivations and unmet needs. Product category affinity, the share of spending in each category, is another widely used dimension, because customers who span several categories are often more valuable than those concentrated in one."}, "KB5": {"chapter": "Chapter 8: Marketing Channels Overview", "section": "Customer Quality Patterns by Channel", "score": 0.16132024, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Channels differ in the type of customer they bring, in ways that tend to be consistent across businesses but are not guaranteed. Referral and email-signup customers frequently repeat more often. Organic search customers often show solid intent. Paid social and discount-led acquisition often bring a broad mix with a higher proportion of one-time buyers. These tendencies are hypotheses to test against each business's own cohort data.\n\nQuality can be examined along several dimensions: first-order value, repeat rate within a fixed window, return rate, discount usage and gross profit after a fixed number of days."}, "KB9": {"chapter": "Chapter 5: Customer Segmentation and Lifetime Value", "section": "Approaches to Segmentation", "score": 0.13386749, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Segmentation divides a customer base into groups that are internally similar and different from one another in ways that matter for marketing. Demographic segmentation uses attributes such as age and location. Behavioral segmentation uses actions such as purchase frequency, categories bought and discount usage. Psychographic segmentation uses attitudes and values. Value-based segmentation ranks customers by their economic contribution.\n\nFor transactional businesses, behavioral and value-based segmentations are the most practical because the required data already exists in the order history. Segments are useful only when they differ in how they are treated. A segmentation that produces groups with identical needs adds complexity without adding insight."}}

