{"KB3": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Availability and Inventory Constraints", "score": 0.08632348, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A product is only worth promoting if it can be shipped. Promoting an item that is about to run out wastes advertising spend and disappoints customers who arrive to find it unavailable. Inventory position, replenishment lead times and planned stock events therefore enter portfolio decisions alongside margin and ratings.\n\nStock events also distort historical analysis. A product that appears to sell poorly during a period may simply have been out of stock for much of it. Comparing sales rates only across days when the product was available gives a cleaner view of underlying demand."}}

