{"KB3": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Availability and Inventory Constraints", "score": 0.08632348, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A product is only worth promoting if it can be shipped. Promoting an item that is about to run out wastes advertising spend and disappoints customers who arrive to find it unavailable. Inventory position, replenishment lead times and planned stock events therefore enter portfolio decisions alongside margin and ratings.\n\nStock events also distort historical analysis. A product that appears to sell poorly during a period may simply have been out of stock for much of it. Comparing sales rates only across days when the product was available gives a cleaner view of underlying demand."}, "KB7": {"chapter": "Chapter 6: Retention, Loyalty and Win-Back", "section": "Replenishment Cycles by Category", "score": 0.0685375, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Products consumed over a predictable period create natural reorder dates. Coffee beans may last three to four weeks, a skincare serum two months, and a pair of running shoes several hundred kilometers of use. Reminder timing based on the expected depletion date typically performs better than reminders sent on a fixed calendar schedule, and the usual cadence of a category forms the reference against which recency is judged."}, "KB8": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Why Products Differ in Their Value to a Business", "score": 0.0675467, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A catalog of twenty products rarely contributes equally to results. Some products generate most of the revenue, some generate most of the profit, and some are loved by the few customers who buy them but are seen by almost nobody. Portfolio thinking treats the catalog as a set of assets with different characteristics, and promotional attention as a scarce resource that is allocated among them.\n\nAttention is scarce because email slots, homepage placements, paid creative and influencer collaborations are limited. Allocating that attention in proportion to historical sales tends to reinforce whatever already sells. Allocating it according to economic value and customer satisfaction can reveal products that deserve more exposure than they currently receive."}, "KB25": {"chapter": "Chapter 4: Pricing, Discounts and Promotions", "section": "Cannibalization and Customer Mix in Promotions", "score": 0.0427222, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Promotion buyers can be divided into new customers, who might not have purchased at all, and existing customers, who might have purchased anyway or soon after. A promotion whose buyers are mostly existing customers has a high cost per incremental order, because most of the discount goes to people who would have purchased at some point. A promotion that brings in many new customers has a different profile, since the value of a new customer extends beyond the first order.\n\nCannibalization also occurs across products: a discount on one item can pull demand away from a similar full-price item. Examining the mix of customers and products inside the promotion window clarifies where the lift came from."}}

