{"KB3": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Availability and Inventory Constraints", "score": 0.08632348, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A product is only worth promoting if it can be shipped. Promoting an item that is about to run out wastes advertising spend and disappoints customers who arrive to find it unavailable. Inventory position, replenishment lead times and planned stock events therefore enter portfolio decisions alongside margin and ratings.\n\nStock events also distort historical analysis. A product that appears to sell poorly during a period may simply have been out of stock for much of it. Comparing sales rates only across days when the product was available gives a cleaner view of underlying demand."}, "KB10": {"chapter": "Chapter 20: Conversion Rate Optimization and Site Experience", "section": "Product Pages", "score": 0.06187599, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Product pages carry the burden of persuasion. Their effectiveness depends on image quality, a clear description of benefits, visible price and shipping information, size or compatibility guidance, customer reviews and answers to common questions. Pages that leave the key uncertainties unresolved tend to produce abandonment and later returns."}, "KB13": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Common Misreadings", "score": 0.05665243, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A common misreading ranks products by revenue alone and concludes that the top of the list deserves the most attention, although revenue conceals margin and satisfaction. Another reads an average rating without the review count. A third treats low sales as evidence of low appeal when low exposure or a stockout is equally plausible."}}

