{"KB1": {"chapter": "Chapter 6: Retention, Loyalty and Win-Back", "section": "Win-Back Design", "score": 0.11596072, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Win-back campaigns reach lapsed customers with the aim of restoring purchasing. Their design involves several choices. Timing relates to how long the customer has been silent compared with their usual cadence. Content can reference the categories and products the customer bought previously. The offer can range from no incentive at all, through free shipping or early access, to a percentage discount.\n\nOffer depth is usually matched to the customer's value and history. Deep discounts are reserved for customers whose expected value justifies them, and suppressed for customers who are likely to return anyway. Because some lapsed customers would return without contact, win-back results are measured against a holdout group that receives no message."}, "KB4": {"chapter": "Chapter 18: Email Marketing in Depth", "section": "Core Automated Flows", "score": 0.07696084, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Automated flows send messages in response to events.\n\n| Flow | Trigger | Typical purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Welcome series | New subscriber | Introduce the brand, encourage the first purchase |\n| Abandoned cart | Items left in cart | Recover purchases that were nearly completed |\n| Post-purchase | Order placed | Confirm, set expectations, cross-sell |\n| Replenishment | Expected depletion date | Prompt reorders of consumables |\n| Review request | Several days after delivery | Collect feedback |\n| Win-back | Silence beyond usual cadence | Restore purchasing |\n\nFlows run continuously and tend to generate a large share of email revenue relative to the effort spent on them, since each message reaches someone at a relevant moment."}, "KB25": {"chapter": "Chapter 4: Pricing, Discounts and Promotions", "section": "Cannibalization and Customer Mix in Promotions", "score": 0.0427222, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Promotion buyers can be divided into new customers, who might not have purchased at all, and existing customers, who might have purchased anyway or soon after. A promotion whose buyers are mostly existing customers has a high cost per incremental order, because most of the discount goes to people who would have purchased at some point. A promotion that brings in many new customers has a different profile, since the value of a new customer extends beyond the first order.\n\nCannibalization also occurs across products: a discount on one item can pull demand away from a similar full-price item. Examining the mix of customers and products inside the promotion window clarifies where the lift came from."}}

