{"KB1": {"chapter": "Chapter 9: Acquisition Economics and Attribution", "section": "Diminishing Returns and Budget Reallocation", "score": 0.53169572, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Spending more in a single channel usually raises cost per acquisition, because the first customers reached are the most responsive. Marginal CAC, the cost of the next customer, therefore exceeds average CAC. Comparisons across channels at average values can hide where the next unit of budget would be most productive.\n\nReallocation decisions therefore consider both average efficiency and marginal return, and are typically made in steps with monitoring, instead of in one large shift, so that effects on volume and cost can be observed."}, "KB5": {"chapter": "Chapter 9: Acquisition Economics and Attribution", "section": "Budget Allocation in Practice", "score": 0.42727721, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Reallocation decisions typically proceed in increments. A modest share of budget is shifted from the weakest channel to a stronger one, results are observed over a full measurement window, and the shift is extended or reversed. Moves are timed with attention to seasonality, so that a holiday peak is not mistaken for the effect of a reallocation."}, "KB7": {"chapter": "Chapter 9: Acquisition Economics and Attribution", "section": "Worked Example: Average Versus Marginal Acquisition Cost", "score": 0.38121954, "is_invented_example": true, "text": "A hypothetical advertiser spends $20,000 in a channel and acquires 500 customers, an average CAC of $40. Raising the budget to $30,000 yields 650 customers in total, an average CAC of $46.15. The extra $10,000 therefore bought only 150 additional customers, a marginal CAC of $66.67.\n\nIf a customer from this channel produces $52 of gross profit in the first ninety days, the average ratio at the higher budget is 52 divided by 46.15, about 1.13, which looks acceptable. The marginal ratio is 52 divided by 66.67, about 0.78, which means the last $10,000 returned less than it cost within that window. The decision to expand a channel therefore turns on the marginal figure, even though average figures are the ones that appear in routine reports."}}

