{"KB5": {"chapter": "Chapter 8: Marketing Channels Overview", "section": "Paid Social", "score": 0.0705596, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Paid social advertising on social platforms reaches people based on interests, demographics and behavior, regardless of whether they are searching for a product. It is mainly a demand-creation channel, introducing products to people who were not looking. Creative quality is a major determinant of performance, and creative fatigue, in which the same advertisement loses effectiveness as audiences see it repeatedly, is a well-known phenomenon.\n\nCustomers acquired through broad social targeting frequently include a higher share of impulse and promotion-driven buyers, and cohort analyses at many businesses show lower repeat rates and higher return rates for this group than for customers who arrive through search or referral. The extent varies by business and is established by measurement."}, "KB12": {"chapter": "Chapter 13: Basket Analysis, Bundling and Cross-Sell", "section": "Cross-Sell Placement", "score": 0.05749328, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Cross-sell opportunities appear on product pages, in the cart, at checkout, in order confirmation messages and in post-purchase email. The timing influences results: recommendations of complementary items shortly after the main purchase are typically more relevant than generic suggestions."}, "KB16": {"chapter": "Chapter 10: Positioning, Messaging and Brand Voice", "section": "Positioning and Value Propositions", "score": 0.05419872, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Positioning defines the place a product occupies in the mind of the target customer relative to alternatives. A positioning statement typically names the target audience, the category, the main benefit and the reason to believe it. A value proposition expresses the promised benefit in a form customers can grasp quickly, and it is only credible when supported by evidence in the product and in the experience of other customers.\n\nGood positioning is specific. Statements that apply equally to every competitor, such as high quality or great value, differentiate nothing. Specific claims, such as a particular weight, a particular use case or a particular guarantee, give customers something to evaluate."}}

