{"KB1": {"chapter": "Chapter 6: Retention, Loyalty and Win-Back", "section": "Win-Back Design", "score": 0.11596072, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Win-back campaigns reach lapsed customers with the aim of restoring purchasing. Their design involves several choices. Timing relates to how long the customer has been silent compared with their usual cadence. Content can reference the categories and products the customer bought previously. The offer can range from no incentive at all, through free shipping or early access, to a percentage discount.\n\nOffer depth is usually matched to the customer's value and history. Deep discounts are reserved for customers whose expected value justifies them, and suppressed for customers who are likely to return anyway. Because some lapsed customers would return without contact, win-back results are measured against a holdout group that receives no message."}, "KB8": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Why Products Differ in Their Value to a Business", "score": 0.0675467, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A catalog of twenty products rarely contributes equally to results. Some products generate most of the revenue, some generate most of the profit, and some are loved by the few customers who buy them but are seen by almost nobody. Portfolio thinking treats the catalog as a set of assets with different characteristics, and promotional attention as a scarce resource that is allocated among them.\n\nAttention is scarce because email slots, homepage placements, paid creative and influencer collaborations are limited. Allocating that attention in proportion to historical sales tends to reinforce whatever already sells. Allocating it according to economic value and customer satisfaction can reveal products that deserve more exposure than they currently receive."}, "KB17": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Combining Signals: A Multi-Criteria View of Product Priority", "score": 0.05261895, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Because no single metric captures product value, portfolio analysis usually combines several. Four signals appear frequently: realized margin, unit volume or share, customer rating with an adequate review count, and availability. Plotting products along two of these dimensions, such as margin against volume, produces quadrants that are given informal names.\n\nHigh-margin, high-volume items are often called stars. High-margin, low-volume items with strong ratings are sometimes called hidden gems or question marks, depending on how well their potential is understood. Low-margin, high-volume items are sometimes called volume traps, because they absorb attention and inventory while contributing less profit than their sales suggest. Low-margin, low-volume items are the laggards. The labels are conventions, and the thresholds that separate the quadrants are set by the analyst, not by the data."}, "KB24": {"chapter": "Chapter 4: Pricing, Discounts and Promotions", "section": "Cannibalization and Customer Mix in Promotions", "score": 0.0433658, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Promotion buyers can be divided into new customers, who might not have purchased at all, and existing customers, who might have purchased anyway or soon after. A promotion whose buyers are mostly existing customers has a high cost per incremental order, because most of the discount goes to people who would have purchased at some point. A promotion that brings in many new customers has a different profile, since the value of a new customer extends beyond the first order.\n\nCannibalization also occurs across products: a discount on one item can pull demand away from a similar full-price item. Examining the mix of customers and products inside the promotion window clarifies where the lift came from."}}

