Relevant handbook guidance (methodology only):
{"KB6": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "List Margin Versus Realized Margin", "score": 0.0685375, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "List margin is calculated from the catalog price: list price minus unit cost, divided by list price. Realized margin is calculated from the price customers actually paid after discounts, codes and promotions. The two can diverge sharply. A product with a healthy list margin of 55 percent that is routinely sold at 30 percent off has a realized margin of roughly 36 percent, since the discount comes entirely out of the margin and the cost of the product is unchanged.\n\nRealized margin requires line-level data that records the price paid for each item. Aggregating from order totals alone hides which products carry the discounts. Products that are frequently featured in discount campaigns, bundled as free gifts or included in welcome offers tend to show a large gap between list and realized margin."}, "KB8": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Why Products Differ in Their Value to a Business", "score": 0.0675467, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A catalog of twenty products rarely contributes equally to results. Some products generate most of the revenue, some generate most of the profit, and some are loved by the few customers who buy them but are seen by almost nobody. Portfolio thinking treats the catalog as a set of assets with different characteristics, and promotional attention as a scarce resource that is allocated among them.\n\nAttention is scarce because email slots, homepage placements, paid creative and influencer collaborations are limited. Allocating that attention in proportion to historical sales tends to reinforce whatever already sells. Allocating it according to economic value and customer satisfaction can reveal products that deserve more exposure than they currently receive."}, "KB13": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Common Misreadings", "score": 0.05665243, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "A common misreading ranks products by revenue alone and concludes that the top of the list deserves the most attention, although revenue conceals margin and satisfaction. Another reads an average rating without the review count. A third treats low sales as evidence of low appeal when low exposure or a stockout is equally plausible."}, "KB17": {"chapter": "Chapter 3: Product Portfolios and Merchandising", "section": "Combining Signals: A Multi-Criteria View of Product Priority", "score": 0.05261895, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Because no single metric captures product value, portfolio analysis usually combines several. Four signals appear frequently: realized margin, unit volume or share, customer rating with an adequate review count, and availability. Plotting products along two of these dimensions, such as margin against volume, produces quadrants that are given informal names.\n\nHigh-margin, high-volume items are often called stars. High-margin, low-volume items with strong ratings are sometimes called hidden gems or question marks, depending on how well their potential is understood. Low-margin, high-volume items are sometimes called volume traps, because they absorb attention and inventory while contributing less profit than their sales suggest. Low-margin, low-volume items are the laggards. The labels are conventions, and the thresholds that separate the quadrants are set by the analyst, not by the data."}}

