{"KB2": {"chapter": "Chapter 7: Voice of the Customer: Reviews, Ratings and Feedback", "section": "Root-Cause Drill-Down", "score": 0.307358, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Dissatisfaction observed at the category level is usually traced through successive cuts: category, then product, then time period, then theme. Each cut asks whether the pattern is concentrated or spread. If most negative reviews in a category come from one product, the issue is product-specific. If the share of negative reviews rises abruptly in a particular month, an event such as a supplier change or a production batch may be responsible.\n\nComparisons between periods are made on the same basis, using the same product, comparable review counts and the same theme definitions. A concentrated, time-bounded pattern is a stronger signal than a diffuse, persistent one."}, "KB4": {"chapter": "Chapter 7: Voice of the Customer: Reviews, Ratings and Feedback", "section": "Product Issues Versus Fulfillment Issues", "score": 0.14804719, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Some negative reviews concern the shipping experience and not the product. Late delivery, damaged packaging and wrong items appear as complaints in reviews of every product and category, and they tend to depress ratings broadly and modestly. Product defects appear as complaints concentrated on particular products.\n\nSeparating the two uses operational data: comparing ratings for orders that arrived on time with those that arrived late, and checking whether the product-related theme persists among on-time orders. A theme that remains frequent among on-time deliveries points to the product itself."}, "KB14": {"chapter": "Chapter 7: Voice of the Customer: Reviews, Ratings and Feedback", "section": "Theme and Topic Analysis of Review Text", "score": 0.08632348, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Theme analysis assigns each review to one or more topics, such as sizing, durability, comfort, value, shipping or a specific component. Methods range from manual coding of a sample, through keyword dictionaries, to topic models and language-model classification. The result is a table of themes with their frequency, which can be computed over all reviews, only negative reviews, or only positive ones.\n\nFrequency among negative reviews indicates what customers complain about most, while frequency among positive reviews indicates what they value. Both are useful: the first points to product or service problems, and the second supplies the language and benefits that can anchor messaging. Because a review can mention more than one theme, percentages can sum to more than one hundred."}, "KB16": {"chapter": "Chapter 7: Voice of the Customer: Reviews, Ratings and Feedback", "section": "Sentiment Versus Topic", "score": 0.07921032, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Sentiment analysis labels text as positive, negative or neutral. Topic analysis labels what the text is about. The two answer different questions. A sentiment score can show that complaints rose, but only topics reveal what the complaints concern. A review that praises comfort and criticizes durability holds both positive and negative sentiment, and the topics separate them."}, "KB21": {"chapter": "Chapter 7: Voice of the Customer: Reviews, Ratings and Feedback", "section": "Detecting Changes Over Time", "score": 0.05749328, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Quality problems frequently begin at a specific date, for example when a new production batch reaches customers. Plotting the monthly or weekly share of negative reviews for a product, and the monthly frequency of the main negative theme, can reveal a step change. Because reviews arrive with a delay after purchase, the visible change in reviews follows the underlying change in the product by some days or weeks."}}

