{"KB4": {"chapter": "Chapter 7: Voice of the Customer: Reviews, Ratings and Feedback", "section": "Product Issues Versus Fulfillment Issues", "score": 0.14804719, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Some negative reviews concern the shipping experience and not the product. Late delivery, damaged packaging and wrong items appear as complaints in reviews of every product and category, and they tend to depress ratings broadly and modestly. Product defects appear as complaints concentrated on particular products.\n\nSeparating the two uses operational data: comparing ratings for orders that arrived on time with those that arrived late, and checking whether the product-related theme persists among on-time orders. A theme that remains frequent among on-time deliveries points to the product itself."}, "KB21": {"chapter": "Chapter 7: Voice of the Customer: Reviews, Ratings and Feedback", "section": "Detecting Changes Over Time", "score": 0.05749328, "is_invented_example": false, "text": "Quality problems frequently begin at a specific date, for example when a new production batch reaches customers. Plotting the monthly or weekly share of negative reviews for a product, and the monthly frequency of the main negative theme, can reveal a step change. Because reviews arrive with a delay after purchase, the visible change in reviews follows the underlying change in the product by some days or weeks."}}

